## Why This Is Asked

Test practical tracing and end-to-end visibility across CNPA during retries and regional outages, ensuring the correlation persists across services and layers.

## Key Concepts

- Correlation IDs / distributed tracing across HTTP, DB, Kafka, Redis
- Middleware propagation, headers, and context propagation
- Retry semantics, idempotent writes, and rollback strategies
- Observability: logs, metrics, traces, dashboards

## Code Example

```javascript
// Express middleware to ensure correlation ID propagation
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const id = req.headers['x-correlation-id'] || uuidv4();
  req.correlationId = id;
  res.setHeader('X-Correlation-Id', id);
  next();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you verify the correlation ID is preserved through PostgreSQL writes and Kafka messages?
- How would you test this under retries and partial outages, including automated end-to-end tests?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of data consistency challenges across CNPA stack components and the ability to design practical solutions for production environments.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end consistency across HTTP API, PostgreSQL, Kafka, and Redis
- Transactional outbox pattern for reliable event publication
- Idempotent producers and consumers to handle retries safely
- Foreign keys and unique constraints to preserve data integrity
- Load testing validation with automated consistency checks and backfill procedures

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-outbox implementation
const signupTransaction = await db.transaction(async (trx) => {
  // Insert user with FK constraints
  const user = await trx('users').insert(userData);

  // Create outbox record in same transaction
  await trx('outbox').insert({
    event_type: 'user_signup',
    payload: { user_id: user.id, ...userData },
    idempotency_key: generateUUID()
  });

  return user;
});
```

